167	\subsection{Problem Formulation and Expert Pool}
168	
169	An \emph{adapter} $g_\phi: \mathbb{R}^{B \times T \times d} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^{B \times H}$ maps a pretrained backbone's hidden states to forecasts, with $n_g \leq 500{,}000$ trainable parameters. We maintain a pool of $K{=}5$ topologically distinct expert adapters (mean-pool, last-token, max-pool, attention-pool, Conv1d-pool), chosen to span complementary pooling strategies over the temporal dimension. An LLM-guided architecture search (Appendix~\ref{app:aas_details}) confirms that richer search spaces yield strictly better single adapters ($-9\%$ MSE; Appendix~\ref{app:aas_results}), motivating expert diversity.
170	
171	\textbf{Standard protocol.} Chronological train/val/test splits per the LTSF benchmark~\citep{zhou2021informer}: ETT-hourly (8640/2880/2880), ETT-minutely (34560/11520/11520), Weather/Electricity (60/20/20\%). Final evaluation uses the held-out test set.
172	
173	\subsection{Raw-Routed Mixture of Adapters (RR-MoA)}
174	\label{sec:rrmoa}
175	
176	Standard MoE routing on TSFM hidden states collapses catastrophically due to gradient co-adaptation (\S\ref{sec:intro}). We propose \textbf{RR-MoA}, which splits the information stream: the TSFM processes deep embeddings $\mathbf{H}$, while a lightweight gate reads the \textit{raw, pre-normalization} input $\mathbf{X}_\text{raw}$ to compute sparse Top-$k$ routing weights:
177	\begin{equation}
178	\mathbf{Y} = \sum_{j \in \mathcal{T}} \underbrace{\widetilde{w}_j}_{\text{sparse weight}} \cdot \underbrace{\text{Expert}_j(\mathbf{H}_\text{FM})}_{\text{deep embedding branch}},\quad
179	\mathcal{T} = \mathrm{TopK}\!\left(G_\psi(\mathbf{X}_\text{raw}),\, k\right),\;\;
180	\widetilde{w} = \mathrm{softmax}_{\mathcal{T}}\!\left(G_\psi(\mathbf{X}_\text{raw})\right)
181	\label{eq:rrmoa}
182	\end{equation}
183	where $G_\psi$ is a small Conv1d\,+\,pooling\,+\,linear gate ($\sim$1.1K params) operating directly on the raw time series, and each $\text{Expert}_k$ is a canonical adapter head. Routing is restricted to the Top-$k$ experts with the highest gate logits, so only $k$ of $K$ experts execute per sample. With $K{=}5$, $k{=}2$ we obtain $40\%$ of the dense FLOPs while retaining $63\%$ of the dense MSE gain (\S\ref{sec:topk}). The raw input preserves the temporal statistics (trend, amplitude, volatility) that RevIN strips away, enabling per-sample routing that reflects the physical characteristics of each time series window.
184	
185	\begin{algorithm}[h]
186	\caption{RR-MoA: Raw-Routed Mixture of Adapters with Top-$k$ sparse routing}
187	\label{alg:rrmoa}
188	\begin{algorithmic}[1]
189	\REQUIRE Strictly frozen TSFM $f_{\theta_\text{frozen}}$, raw input $\mathbf{X}_\text{raw}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times L}$, $K$ expert adapters, sparsity $k\leq K$
190	\STATE $\mathbf{H}\leftarrow f_{\theta_\text{frozen}}(\mathbf{X}_\text{raw})$ \hfill \COMMENT{Deep embeddings; \textbf{no gradients} through $\theta$}
191	\STATE $\mathbf{g}\leftarrow G_\psi(\mathbf{X}_\text{raw})$ \hfill \COMMENT{Raw-signal gate logits, $\mathbf{g}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times K}$}
192	\STATE $\mathcal{T}\leftarrow \mathrm{TopK}(\mathbf{g},\,k)$ \hfill \COMMENT{Per-sample indices of the $k$ selected experts}
193	\STATE $\widetilde{\mathbf{w}}\leftarrow \mathrm{softmax}_{\mathcal{T}}(\mathbf{g})$ \hfill \COMMENT{Renormalized over selected experts only}
194	\STATE \textbf{for each}\ $j \in \mathcal{T}$\ \textbf{do}
195	\STATE \quad $\mathbf{o}_j\leftarrow \text{Expert}_j(\mathbf{H})$ \hfill \COMMENT{Only the $k$ selected experts execute}
196	\STATE \textbf{end for}
197	\STATE $\mathbf{Y}\leftarrow \sum_{j\in\mathcal{T}}\widetilde{w}_j\cdot \mathbf{o}_j$
198	\RETURN $\mathbf{Y}$
199	\end{algorithmic}
200	\end{algorithm}
201	
202	\section{Experiments}
203	
204	\subsection{Setup}
205	
206	\textbf{Backbones.} Our primary backbone is MOMENT-small~\citep{goswami2024moment} ($d_\text{model}{=}512$, 8 T5-style encoder blocks, internal RevIN~\citep{kim2021revin}). We additionally evaluate MOMENT-large ($d_\text{model}{=}1024$, 24 blocks), Moirai-1.1-R-small~\citep{woo2024moirai} ($d_\text{model}{=}384$, 6 blocks, no RevIN), and Chronos-T5-small~\citep{ansari2024chronos} ($d_\text{model}{=}512$, 6 T5 encoder blocks, no RevIN), testing cross-backbone generalization across both encoder-only and encoder-decoder architectures in \S\ref{sec:cross_backbone}. Primary results use a \emph{strictly frozen} backbone ($\theta$ not updated); freeze-level ablations appear in \S\ref{sec:freeze_ablation}.
207	
208	\textbf{MSE scale.} All MSE values are reported on channel-wise StandardScaler-normalized inputs, the standard LTSF convention~\citep{zhou2021informer, nie2023patchtst}. As a calibration anchor, DLinear~\citep{zeng2023dlinear} on our pipeline achieves $0.416$ (ETTh1), $0.322$ (ETTm1), $0.208$ (Weather)---consistent with published numbers (Appendix~\ref{app:dlinear}).
209	
210	\textbf{Datasets and tasks.} ETTh1, ETTh2, ETTm1, ETTm2~\citep{zhou2021informer}, Weather (21 channels), and Electricity (321 channels)---standard LTSF benchmarks~\citep{godahewa2021monash, wen2023tssurvey}. Chronological train/val/test splits follow the LTSF protocol. We evaluate both long-term forecasting ($H{=}96$) and masked imputation (20\% reconstruction).
211	
212	\textbf{Training.} Adam optimizer, lr$=10^{-3}$, MSE loss, batch size 128, 15 epochs. Final reported numbers are 15-epoch test MSE. Multi-seed experiments use seeds $\{42, 43, 44, 45, 46\}$ ($n{=}5$) for the core table; ablations use seeds $\{42, 43, 44\}$.
213	
214	\textbf{Baselines.} We compare against seven baselines: (i)~three hand-designed \textit{fixed adapters} (linear, attention, conv---representing standard TSFM practice), (ii)~\textit{LoRA}~\citep{hu2022lora} (108-run sweep over rank, targets, and heads; Appendix~\ref{app:lora_sweep}), (iii)~a \textit{TRACE-style} multi-scale adapter~\citep{li2025trace} (multi-scale convolutions + gated importance weighting; no public code available, we implement the core ideas), (iv)~an \textit{independent ensemble} of the same 5 expert heads trained separately and averaged at inference (isolating routing from expert diversity), (v)~\textit{AdaMix}~\citep{wang2022adamix} (standard hidden-state MoE routing), (vi)~\textit{full fine-tuning} (all 8 encoder blocks unfrozen, best of 5 heads $\times$ 2 learning rates---the strongest non-routing baseline), and (vii)~\textit{DLinear}~\citep{zeng2023dlinear} as a supervised calibration anchor (not a competitor). Items (i)--(v) use the identical strictly frozen protocol; full fine-tuning unfreezes all backbone layers.
215	
216	\textbf{Reproducibility.} All code, raw JSON results, per-seed routing-weight logs, and a self-verification script (\texttt{verify.py}) that re-derives every numerical claim from raw data are included in the supplementary material and will be open-sourced upon acceptance.
