291	\textbf{AdaMix} collapses to a single expert whenever backbone layers are unfrozen: routing entropy drops to exactly $0.000\pm0.000$ in 5 of 12 unfrozen configurations, and remains below $0.55$ in most others. Under the strictly frozen setting entropy recovers ($0.3$--$1.0$), but MSE is still dominated by RR-MoA. \textbf{Controlled RevIN ablation.} The green rows in Table~\ref{tab:adamix} show AdaMix on MOMENT with RevIN \emph{disabled} (replaced by an identity function). Under identical last-4 unfreezing, routing entropy recovers from $0.000$ to $0.66$--$1.32$ on all three datasets. This controlled experiment---same backbone, same unfreezing, only RevIN removed---proves that normalization is the \emph{causal} mechanism behind routing collapse. \textbf{Normalization generalization.} Replacing RevIN with BatchNorm1d or GroupNorm produces the same collapse (entropy $0.004$ and $0.000$ respectively at step 400; Appendix~\ref{app:norm_generalization}), confirming that the failure is a general property of instance-level normalization, not a RevIN-specific artifact. Figure~\ref{fig:trajectory} provides per-step mechanistic evidence: under unfreezing with any normalizer (red), entropy collapses within ${\sim}50$ gradient steps; without normalization (green), entropy remains stable at $0.82$.
