352	\textbf{Why RevIN destroys routing: formal analysis.} Each input window $\mathbf{x}_i$ decomposes into mean $M_i = \mu(\mathbf{x}_i)$, scale $\Sigma_i = \sigma(\mathbf{x}_i)$, and shape $\mathbf{S}_i = (\mathbf{x}_i - M_i)/\Sigma_i$. RevIN maps $\mathbf{x}_i \mapsto \mathbf{S}_i$, discarding $(M_i, \Sigma_i)$.
353	
354	\begin{proposition}[Routing Information Loss Under RevIN]
355	\label{thm:mi_decomp}
356	Let $X$ be a random input window, $E \in \{1,\ldots,K\}$ the expert assignment produced by any deterministic router, and $S = (X - M)/\Sigma$ the RevIN-normalized shape.
357	\begin{enumerate}
358	    \item[\textup{(i)}] $I(X; E) \geq I(S; E)$ \hfill\textup{(Data Processing Inequality)}
359	    \item[\textup{(ii)}] If $(M, \Sigma) \perp\!\!\!\perp S$, then $I(X; E) - I(S; E) \geq I(M, \Sigma;\, E)$, with equality iff $(M,\Sigma) \perp\!\!\!\perp S \mid E$.
360	    \item[\textup{(iii)}] If additionally $E \perp\!\!\!\perp S \mid (M, \Sigma)$, then $I(X; E) - I(S; E) = H(E)$.
361	\end{enumerate}
362	\end{proposition}
363	
364	Part~(i) is the DPI applied to $X \to S \to E$. Part~(ii) uses the chain rule: $I(X;E) = I(S;E) + I(M,\Sigma; E \mid S)$; under $(M,\Sigma) \perp\!\!\!\perp S$ this simplifies to $I(S;E) + I(M,\Sigma; E) + I(M,\Sigma; S \mid E)$, giving a lower bound since $I(M,\Sigma; S \mid E) \geq 0$. The residual term $I(M,\Sigma; S \mid E)$ captures the \emph{collider effect}: conditioning on the expert assignment $E$ can induce dependence between otherwise independent statistics and shape, meaning RevIN may destroy \emph{more} routing information than $I(M,\Sigma; E)$ alone. Part~(iii) shows that when routing depends \emph{entirely} on the stripped statistics, RevIN destroys \emph{all} routing information ($H(E)$ nats). Full proof in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}. The independence $(M,\Sigma) \perp\!\!\!\perp S$ is RevIN's design assumption; $E \perp\!\!\!\perp S \mid (M,\Sigma)$ is validated by Figure~\ref{fig:routing_viz}, where expert selection clusters by amplitude and volatility regardless of waveform shape.
365	
366	\textbf{Quantitative prediction.} Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp} implies that datasets where RevIN strips more information---i.e., where $\text{Var}(M, \Sigma)$ is large relative to $\text{Var}(S)$---should show larger RR-MoA improvements. We define the \emph{routing signal ratio} $R(\mathcal{D}) = [\text{Var}(M) + \text{Var}(\Sigma)] / \overline{\text{Var}(S)}$ and compute it for each dataset. Figure~\ref{fig:signal_ratio} validates this prediction across all seven LTSF benchmarks: $R$ correlates with the RR-MoA improvement $\Delta\%$ with Spearman $\rho = -0.96$ ($p < 0.001$; 95\% bootstrap CI: $[-1.00, -0.68]$; permutation $p = 0.0025$; leave-one-out minimum $\rho = -0.94$). Crucially, Traffic---the dataset with the lowest $R{=}0.14$---is correctly predicted as the boundary case where raw routing provides no advantage ($\Delta{=}{+}2.9\%$), demonstrating that the theory identifies not only \emph{where} raw routing helps but also \emph{where it does not}.
367	
368	This explains all empirical observations: (i)~the RevIN router loses $60$--$88\%$ MSE because it loses $I(M,\Sigma; E)$; (ii)~Moirai (no RevIN) retains routing diversity because the gate accesses $(M, \Sigma, S)$; (iii)~Figure~\ref{fig:routing_viz} confirms expert selection correlates with amplitude and volatility. When backbone layers are unfrozen, the co-adaptation loop (Figure~\ref{fig:trajectory}) amplifies the signal poverty: the dominant expert's gradient grows while others starve, collapsing to a single expert within ${\sim}50$ steps.
369	
370	\textbf{What the router learns.} Per-sample routing decisions correlate with amplitude and volatility---the very statistics RevIN strips (Figure~\ref{fig:routing_viz} in Appendix~\ref{app:routing_weights}). On ETTh1, Expert~0 (mean-pool) serves as the generalist ($53\%$), while Expert~2 (max-pool) handles low-amplitude windows and Expert~3 (attention-pool) handles high-amplitude windows. The router reserves specialized processing for windows at the distribution tails.
