\textbf{RR-MoA} maintains routing entropy $1.0$--$1.57$ (max $1.609$) and wins \textbf{54/54} against the best fixed adapter on 6 datasets, with improvements from $-18\%$ to $-77\%$. On Traffic (7th dataset), RR-MoA does not improve over the conv baseline---consistent with Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}'s prediction, since Traffic has the lowest routing signal ratio ($R{=}0.14$; Figure~\ref{fig:signal_ratio}). The contrast with AdaMix is stark: raw-signal routing is immune to gradient co-adaptation.

\textbf{Comprehensive baseline comparison.} Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares RR-MoA against all baselines under the identical strictly frozen backbone protocol on MOMENT-small.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{\textbf{Comprehensive baseline comparison} (MOMENT-small, Top-2, 5 seeds, test MSE mean$\pm$std, H=96). Items above the double rule are strictly frozen; full fine-tuning unfreezes all 8 encoder blocks (best of 5 heads $\times$ 2 learning rates). Significance: Wilcoxon signed-rank pooled across 6 datasets$\times$5 seeds, Bonferroni-corrected for 6 comparisons. DLinear is a supervised calibration anchor.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
Method & ETTh1 & ETTm1 & Weather & $p$-value \\
\midrule
Best fixed adapter & $1.254 \pm 0.026$ & $1.148 \pm 0.035$ & $0.528 \pm 0.018$ & ${<}0.001^{***}$ \\
Best LoRA (108-run sweep) & $1.135 \pm 0.014$ & $0.895 \pm 0.039$ & $0.575 \pm 0.016$ & ${<}0.010^{**}$ \\
TRACE (frozen) & $1.136 \pm 0.070$ & $1.005 \pm 0.057$ & $0.425 \pm 0.003$ & ${<}0.001^{***}$ \\
Ind.\ Ensemble (5 experts) & $1.081 \pm 0.021$ & $1.049 \pm 0.032$ & $0.509 \pm 0.024$ & ${<}0.001^{***}$ \\
AdaMix (frozen) & $1.109 \pm 0.031$ & $1.008 \pm 0.012$ & $0.459 \pm 0.017$ & ${<}0.001^{***}$ \\
\midrule
Full fine-tuning (all unfrozen) & $1.060 \pm 0.059$ & $0.871 \pm 0.029$ & $0.468 \pm 0.009$ & ${<}0.001^{***}$ \\
\midrule
\textbf{RR-MoA (Top-2, frozen)} & $\mathbf{0.680 \pm 0.027}$ & $\mathbf{0.564 \pm 0.059}$ & $\mathbf{0.276 \pm 0.017}$ & --- \\
\midrule
\rowcolor{gray!8} DLinear (from scratch) & $0.416 \pm 0.002$ & $0.322 \pm 0.004$ & $0.208 \pm 0.003$ & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

RR-MoA significantly outperforms every baseline ($p{<}0.001$ for 5 of 6, $p{<}0.01$ for LoRA; Wilcoxon signed-rank, Bonferroni-corrected across 6 datasets$\times$5 seeds). Most strikingly, \textbf{frozen RR-MoA beats full fine-tuning} (all layers unfrozen) by $16$--$77\%$ across all 6 datasets (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}), decisively confirming the Frozen Paradox: unfreezing the backbone destroys routing diversity via gradient co-adaptation (Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen}), making the additional expressivity counterproductive. The independent ensemble---same experts, no router---is $36$--$46\%$ worse, confirming that learned per-sample routing adds substantial value beyond expert diversity alone. LoRA with last-4 unfreezing (Appendix~\ref{app:lora_sweep}) shows no improvement over frozen LoRA ($1.154$ vs $1.154$ on ETTh1), confirming that LoRA operates in the same normalized representation space where the signal is already degraded.

\textbf{Supervised calibration anchor.} DLinear~\citep{zeng2023dlinear} trained from scratch achieves $0.416$ (ETTh1), $0.322$ (ETTm1), $0.208$ (Weather). On MOMENT-small, this gap is $40$--$60\%$; however, on Moirai the gap narrows dramatically (Weather: $0.209$ vs $0.208$; \S\ref{sec:cross_backbone}).

\textbf{Rawness vs.\ bypass.} To isolate whether RR-MoA's advantage comes from bypassing the backbone or specifically from reading the raw pre-normalization signal, we replace the router's input with the same signal after per-window RevIN (zero mean, unit variance). Everything else is identical.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{\textbf{Rawness vs.\ bypass ablation} (strictly frozen, Top-2, 3 seeds, test MSE mean$\pm$std). The RevIN router reads the same input after per-window normalization. Stripping temporal statistics degrades MSE by 60--88\%, confirming the router relies on the statistics RevIN removes.}
\label{tab:router_input}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & Raw router (main) & RevIN router & Degradation vs Raw ($\Delta\%$, $\uparrow$ worse) \\
\midrule
ETTh1   & $\mathbf{0.690 \pm 0.021}$ & $1.101 \pm 0.008$ & $\mathbf{+59.6\%}$ \\
ETTm1   & $\mathbf{0.572 \pm 0.073}$ & $1.077 \pm 0.020$ & $\mathbf{+88.3\%}$ \\
Weather & $\mathbf{0.289 \pm 0.008}$ & $0.542 \pm 0.026$ & $\mathbf{+87.5\%}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The RevIN router suffers $60$--$88\%$ MSE degradation on every dataset: bypass alone is insufficient; the \emph{content} of the raw signal matters. Routing entropy remains high ($1.50$--$1.57$), but the gate can no longer make discriminative decisions without per-window temporal statistics.

\textbf{Why RevIN destroys routing: formal analysis.} Each input window $\mathbf{x}_i$ decomposes into mean $M_i = \mu(\mathbf{x}_i)$, scale $\Sigma_i = \sigma(\mathbf{x}_i)$, and shape $\mathbf{S}_i = (\mathbf{x}_i - M_i)/\Sigma_i$. RevIN maps $\mathbf{x}_i \mapsto \mathbf{S}_i$, discarding $(M_i, \Sigma_i)$.

\begin{proposition}[Routing Information Loss Under RevIN]
\label{thm:mi_decomp}
Let $X$ be a random input window, $E \in \{1,\ldots,K\}$ the expert assignment produced by any deterministic router, and $S = (X - M)/\Sigma$ the RevIN-normalized shape.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[\textup{(i)}] $I(X; E) \geq I(S; E)$ \hfill\textup{(Data Processing Inequality)}
    \item[\textup{(ii)}] If $(M, \Sigma) \perp\!\!\!\perp S$, then $I(X; E) - I(S; E) \geq I(M, \Sigma;\, E)$, with equality iff $(M,\Sigma) \perp\!\!\!\perp S \mid E$.
    \item[\textup{(iii)}] If additionally $E \perp\!\!\!\perp S \mid (M, \Sigma)$, then $I(X; E) - I(S; E) = H(E)$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

Part~(i) is the DPI applied to $X \to S \to E$. Part~(ii) uses the chain rule: $I(X;E) = I(S;E) + I(M,\Sigma; E \mid S)$; under $(M,\Sigma) \perp\!\!\!\perp S$ this simplifies to $I(S;E) + I(M,\Sigma; E) + I(M,\Sigma; S \mid E)$, giving a lower bound since $I(M,\Sigma; S \mid E) \geq 0$. The residual term $I(M,\Sigma; S \mid E)$ captures the \emph{collider effect}: conditioning on the expert assignment $E$ can induce dependence between otherwise independent statistics and shape, meaning RevIN may destroy \emph{more} routing information than $I(M,\Sigma; E)$ alone. Part~(iii) shows that when routing depends \emph{entirely} on the stripped statistics, RevIN destroys \emph{all} routing information ($H(E)$ nats). Full proof in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}. The independence $(M,\Sigma) \perp\!\!\!\perp S$ is RevIN's design assumption; $E \perp\!\!\!\perp S \mid (M,\Sigma)$ is validated by Figure~\ref{fig:routing_viz}, where expert selection clusters by amplitude and volatility regardless of waveform shape.

\textbf{Quantitative prediction.} Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp} implies that datasets where RevIN strips more information---i.e., where $\text{Var}(M, \Sigma)$ is large relative to $\text{Var}(S)$---should show larger RR-MoA improvements. We define the \emph{routing signal ratio} $R(\mathcal{D}) = [\text{Var}(M) + \text{Var}(\Sigma)] / \overline{\text{Var}(S)}$ and compute it for each dataset. Figure~\ref{fig:signal_ratio} validates this prediction across all seven LTSF benchmarks: $R$ correlates with the RR-MoA improvement $\Delta\%$ with Spearman $\rho = -0.96$ ($p < 0.001$; 95\% bootstrap CI: $[-1.00, -0.68]$; permutation $p = 0.0025$; leave-one-out minimum $\rho = -0.94$). Crucially, Traffic---the dataset with the lowest $R{=}0.14$---is correctly predicted as the boundary case where raw routing provides no advantage ($\Delta{=}{+}2.9\%$), demonstrating that the theory identifies not only \emph{where} raw routing helps but also \emph{where it does not}.

This explains all empirical observations: (i)~the RevIN router loses $60$--$88\%$ MSE because it loses $I(M,\Sigma; E)$; (ii)~Moirai (no RevIN) retains routing diversity because the gate accesses $(M, \Sigma, S)$; (iii)~Figure~\ref{fig:routing_viz} confirms expert selection correlates with amplitude and volatility. When backbone layers are unfrozen, the co-adaptation loop (Figure~\ref{fig:trajectory}) amplifies the signal poverty: the dominant expert's gradient grows while others starve, collapsing to a single expert within ${\sim}50$ steps.

\textbf{What the router learns.} Per-sample routing decisions correlate with amplitude and volatility---the very statistics RevIN strips (Figure~\ref{fig:routing_viz} in Appendix~\ref{app:routing_weights}). On ETTh1, Expert~0 (mean-pool) serves as the generalist ($53\%$), while Expert~2 (max-pool) handles low-amplitude windows and Expert~3 (attention-pool) handles high-amplitude windows. The router reserves specialized processing for windows at the distribution tails.

