\subsection{Cross-Modality Control: Vision MoE}
\label{app:vision_moe}

To test whether normalization-induced routing collapse transfers beyond time series, we construct a controlled vision experiment: ResNet-18 (pretrained on ImageNet, adapted for CIFAR-10) as backbone, with $K{=}5$ architecturally diverse expert classifiers (mean-pool, max-pool, attention-pool, conv-pool, last-patch---matching the time-series expert pool), no load-balancing loss, and the same four conditions tested in the time-series experiments.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{\textbf{Vision MoE routing collapse control} (ResNet-18 + CIFAR-10, $K{=}5$ diverse experts, 20 epochs, seed 42). Gradient co-adaptation (Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen}) is confirmed: the unnormalized unfrozen backbone collapses (C). However, adding InstanceNorm2d or BatchNorm2d \emph{prevents} collapse (A, D)---confirming Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}'s prediction that collapse requires the stripped statistics to carry routing signal.}
\label{tab:vision_moe}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llccc@{}}
\toprule
Condition & Configuration & Entropy (final) & Collapses? & Test Acc \\
\midrule
A & Unfrozen + InstanceNorm2d & $1.491$ & No & $0.920$ \\
B & Frozen + InstanceNorm2d   & $0.973$ & No & $0.571$ \\
C & Unfrozen + No norm        & $\mathbf{0.075}$ & \textbf{Yes} & $0.921$ \\
D & Unfrozen + BatchNorm2d    & $1.483$ & No & $0.918$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Key finding: normalization prevents rather than causes collapse in vision.} Without normalization (C), the unfrozen backbone freely co-adapts with the dominant expert, collapsing routing entropy to $0.075$ (vs.\ max $1.609$)---directly confirming the gradient co-adaptation mechanism of Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen}. However, adding InstanceNorm2d (A) or BatchNorm2d (D) \emph{prevents} collapse (entropy $1.49$/$1.48$). This is the \emph{opposite} of time series, where normalization enables collapse.

\textbf{Explanation via Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}.} In time series, per-window mean $M$ and scale $\Sigma$ are the primary routing signal ($\rho{=}{-}0.96$); RevIN strips exactly these statistics. In vision, routing-discriminative information resides in \emph{spatial feature patterns}, not in per-channel spatial statistics. InstanceNorm2d strips per-channel spatial mean and variance---which carry negligible routing information for image classification. This is analogous to Traffic (the time-series dataset with lowest routing signal ratio $R{=}0.14$, where RR-MoA provides no advantage). The collapse mechanism (Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen}) is modality-general; the normalization \emph{trigger} (Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}) depends on whether the stripped statistics overlap with the routing signal.

\section{Factorial Decomposition}

