\section{Conclusion}

We identified \emph{normalization-induced co-adaptation} as the cause of MoE routing collapse on TSFMs, proved via three converging controls and formalized via Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp} ($\rho{=}{-}0.96$, $p{<}0.001$ across seven datasets). \textbf{RR-MoA} breaks the collapse by routing on raw pre-normalization signal with a frozen backbone (\textbf{54/54 wins}, $p{<}0.001$, four backbones, four horizons, two tasks). The \emph{Frozen Paradox}---frozen RR-MoA outperforms full fine-tuning by $16$--$77\%$---reverses the standard PEFT heuristic. The collapse generalizes beyond RevIN to BatchNorm1d and GroupNorm (Appendix~\ref{app:norm_generalization}), predicting analogous failures wherever instance normalization precedes routing in vision MoE and diffusion models.

\textbf{Limitations.} (1)~Absolute MSE gap to supervised baselines on MOMENT-small (narrows with horizon and backbone quality; Appendix~\ref{app:diagnostic} confirms genuine information loss). (2)~Native MoE backbones (Moirai-MoE, Time-MoE) not evaluated. (3)~Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp} proved for a linear model; nonlinear case open. (4)~Normalization generalization validated on time series only. (5)~Expert pool hand-selected; cross-channel routing unexplored.

\label{page:end_main}
