\textbf{Cross-backbone generalization.}\label{sec:cross_backbone} We evaluate RR-MoA on three backbones with instance normalization and one \emph{negative control} without it, revealing a \emph{normalization spectrum}. MOMENT-small/large (RevIN): \textbf{all 18 configs} win per backbone ($27$--$81\%$ improvement). Moirai (no RevIN, LayerNorm only): \textbf{18/18} wins ($3$--$31\%$; Moirai+RR-MoA matches DLinear on Weather: $0.209$ vs $0.208$). \textbf{Negative control---Chronos} (no instance normalization, encoder-decoder): standard AdaMix routing retains high entropy ($1.48$--$1.60$) with \emph{no collapse}, and RR-MoA provides no advantage (only \textbf{1/9} wins). This is exactly as Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp} predicts: without instance normalization, temporal statistics are preserved in the hidden states and pre-normalization routing is unnecessary. The improvement gradient $81\%{>}31\%{>}{\sim}0\%$ tracks normalization aggressiveness, not backbone size (full tables in Appendix~\ref{app:cross_backbone}).

\textbf{Sparse routing, multi-horizon, and task generalization.}\label{sec:topk}
Top-2 sparse routing adds $4.3$\,ms ($+10\%$) latency over a single adapter (Appendix~\ref{app:topk},\ref{app:benchmark}). RR-MoA wins \textbf{24/24} horizon configurations ($H{\in}\{96,192,336,720\}$, $-38\%$ to $-51\%$); the DLinear gap narrows with $H$ ($+83\%{\to}+48\%$ on ETTh1, $+58\%{\to}+14\%$ on Weather; Appendix Table~\ref{tab:horizon}). RR-MoA also generalizes to imputation ($-44\%$ to $-67\%$ on 3/3 datasets; Appendix~\ref{app:imputation}). An information content diagnostic (Appendix~\ref{app:diagnostic}) confirms the DLinear gap reflects genuine information destruction by normalization and patch compression: k-NN on backbone features is $16$--$33\%$ worse than on raw input, and DLinear dominates at every training set size $N{\in}\{10,\ldots,5000\}$.

\section{Conclusion}

We identified \emph{normalization-induced co-adaptation} as the cause of MoE routing collapse on TSFMs, proved via three converging controls and formalized via Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp} ($\rho{=}{-}0.96$, $p{<}0.001$ across seven datasets). \textbf{RR-MoA} breaks the collapse by routing on raw pre-normalization signal with a frozen backbone (\textbf{54/54 wins}, $p{<}0.001$, four backbones, four horizons, two tasks). The \emph{Frozen Paradox}---frozen RR-MoA outperforms full fine-tuning by $16$--$77\%$---reverses the standard PEFT heuristic. The collapse generalizes beyond RevIN to BatchNorm1d and GroupNorm (Appendix~\ref{app:norm_generalization}). A cross-modality control on ResNet-18 + CIFAR-10 (Appendix~\ref{app:vision_moe}) confirms that gradient co-adaptation is modality-general but the normalization trigger is modality-specific: in vision, normalization \emph{prevents} collapse because per-channel spatial statistics carry negligible routing signal---exactly as Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp} predicts.

\textbf{Limitations.} (1)~Absolute MSE gap to supervised baselines on MOMENT-small (narrows with horizon and backbone quality; Appendix~\ref{app:diagnostic} confirms genuine information loss). (2)~Native MoE backbones (Moirai-MoE, Time-MoE) not evaluated. (3)~Proposition~\ref{thm:mi_decomp} proved for a linear model; nonlinear case open. (4)~Normalization generalization validated on time series only. (5)~Expert pool hand-selected; cross-channel routing unexplored.

